<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowStateChanged|typeWindowContentChanged|typeViewScrolled|typeViewClicked|typeNotificationStateChanged"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagReportViewIds|flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_description"
    android:notificationTimeout="100" />
<!--
  ⚠️ notificationTimeout WAS 0, AND 0 IS "SEND ME EVERY SINGLE EVENT, IMMEDIATELY".

  That is the tap the Play Store bug came out of. A big app redrawing itself - the Play Store
  working through a download queue, a Compose list settling - emits window-content-changed
  events continuously, and at 0 the framework forwards every one of them. Each arriving event
  makes this service read the screen, and reading the screen means blocking cross-process
  calls into the app that is busy (see the note on pageMatches in AccessibilityService.kt).

  100ms tells the framework to coalesce: at most one event of a given type per 100ms. It is
  the cheapest fix in the whole change, because it removes the work rather than budgeting it.

  The cost is bounded and small: a block can land up to 100ms later than it used to. Content
  changes were already gated at BLOCK_INTERVAL_MS (200ms), so for those it costs nothing at
  all; only the "you just opened something" window-state event is affected, and a tenth of a
  second is under the app-launch animation.
-->
